1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,683
Erm ...

2
00:00:00,683 --> 00:00:02,912
hello Professor, l'm John Wishart.

3
00:00:02,912 --> 00:00:05,463
I'm working on my entry for the Global Design Competition.

4
00:00:05,463 --> 00:00:09,560
My tutor said you might be able to help me with it.

5
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:12,076
Ah, yes, I got a copy of your drawings.

6
00:00:12,076 --> 00:00:14,033
Come in and tell me about it.

7
00:00:14,033 --> 00:00:15,710
What sort of competition is it?

8
00:00:15,710 --> 00:00:20,737
Well, it's an international design competition and we have to come up with a

9
00:00:20,737 --> 00:00:23,610
new design for a typical domestic kitchen appliance.

10
00:00:23,610 --> 00:00:24,286
I see,

11
00:00:24,286 --> 00:00:26,315
and are there any special conditions?

12
00:00:26,315 --> 00:00:29,020
Does it have to save energy for example?

13
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:32,478
Actually that was the focus in last year's competition.

14
00:00:32,478 --> 00:00:33,630
This year's different.

15
00:00:33,630 --> 00:00:38,223
We have to adopt an innovative approach to existing technology, using it in a

16
00:00:38,223 --> 00:00:40,520
way that hasn't been thought of before.

17
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:41,273
I see,

18
00:00:41,273 --> 00:00:42,403
that sounds tricky.

19
00:00:42,403 --> 00:00:45,040
And what kitchen appliance have you chosen?

20
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,920
Well, I decided to choose the dishwasher.

21
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:50,900
Interesting, what made you choose that?

22
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:56,414
Well, they're an everyday kitchen appliance in most Australian houses but they're all pretty boring

23
00:00:56,414 --> 00:00:58,620
and almost identical to each other.

24
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:02,754
I think some people will be prepared to pay a little extra for something

25
00:01:02,754 --> 00:01:03,640
that looks different.

26
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:05,203
That's a nice idea.

27
00:01:05,203 --> 00:01:08,328
I see you've called your design 'the Rockpool';

28
00:01:08,328 --> 00:01:09,500
why is that?

29
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:13,260
Basically because it looks like the rock pools you find on a beach.

30
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:17,310
The top is made of glass so that you can look down into it.

31
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:19,381
And there's a stone at the bottom.

32
00:01:19,381 --> 00:01:20,860
Is that just for decoration?

33
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:23,016
Actually it does have a function.

34
00:01:23,016 --> 00:01:26,250
Instead of pushing a button, you turn the stone.

35
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:30,210
So it's really just a novel way of starting the dishwasher.

36
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:31,100
That's right.

37
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:33,060
It's a really nice design,

38
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:35,020
but what makes it innovative?

39
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:39,910
Well, I decided to make a dishwasher that uses carbon dioxide.

40
00:01:39,910 --> 00:01:41,863
In place of water and detergent?

41
00:01:41,863 --> 00:01:43,490
How will you manage that?

42
00:01:43,490 --> 00:01:48,096
The idea is to pressurise the carbon dioxide so that it becomes a liquid.

43
00:01:48,096 --> 00:01:52,702
The fluid is then released into the dishwasher where it cleans the dishes all

44
00:01:52,702 --> 00:01:53,360
by itself.

45
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:55,258
Sounds like a brilliant idea!

46
00:01:55,258 --> 00:01:59,813
Your system will totally do away with the need for strong detergents.

47
00:01:59,813 --> 00:02:02,850
So what happens once the dishes are clean?

48
00:02:02,850 --> 00:02:06,311
Well, to allow them to dry, the liquid carbon dioxide

49
00:02:06,311 --> 00:02:10,810
and the waste materials all go to an area called the holding chamber.

50
00:02:10,810 --> 00:02:14,753
That's where the liquid is depressurised and so it reverts to a gas.

51
00:02:14,753 --> 00:02:19,000
Then the oil and grease are separated out and sent to the waste system.

52
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,250
It sounds like you've thought it all out very thoroughly.

53
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:26,758
So, what happens to the carbon dioxide once the process is complete?

54
00:02:26,758 --> 00:02:28,260
Not wasted I hope.

55
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:28,592
Actually,

56
00:02:28,592 --> 00:02:30,920
that's where the real savings are made.

57
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:35,908
The carbon dioxide is sent back to the cylinder and can be used again

58
00:02:35,908 --> 00:02:36,620
and again.

59
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:37,880
What a terrific idea.

60
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:40,400
Do you think it will ever be built?

61
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:41,344
Probably not,

62
00:02:41,344 --> 00:02:42,760
but that's OK.

63
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:47,540
Well, I'm sure a lot of positive things will come out of your design.

64
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:51,718
Now, you seem to have thought about everything so what exactly did you need

65
00:02:51,718 --> 00:02:53,210
me to help you with?

66
00:02:53,210 --> 00:02:57,005
Well, my design has made it to the final stage of the competition

67
00:02:57,005 --> 00:02:58,756
and, in a few months' time.

68
00:02:58,756 --> 00:03:00,507
I have to give a presentation,

69
00:03:00,507 --> 00:03:04,010
and that's the part I was hoping you could help me with.

70
00:03:04,010 --> 00:03:06,133
Right, well that should be easy enough.

71
00:03:06,133 --> 00:03:08,560
What have you managed to do so far?

72
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:11,937
Well, I've got detailed drawings to show how it will work

73
00:03:11,937 --> 00:03:14,700
and I've also written a 500-word paper on it.

74
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:15,229
I see.

75
00:03:15,229 --> 00:03:18,929
Well, if you want to stand a good chance of winning you really need

76
00:03:18,929 --> 00:03:20,250
a model of the machine.

77
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:23,440
Yes, I thought I might but l'm having a few problems.

78
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:25,835
What is the main difficulty so far?

79
00:03:25,835 --> 00:03:28,230
Let me guess - is it the materials?

80
00:03:28,230 --> 00:03:28,609
Yes.

81
00:03:28,609 --> 00:03:33,911
I want it to look professional but everything that's top quality is also very

82
00:03:33,911 --> 00:03:34,290
expensive.

83
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:37,512
Look, projects like this are very important to us.

84
00:03:37,512 --> 00:03:39,660
They really help lift our profile.

85
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:42,573
So why don't you talk to the university about a grant?

86
00:03:42,573 --> 00:03:45,750
I can help you fill out the application forms if you like.

87
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:47,070
That would be great.

88
00:03:47,070 --> 00:03:50,167
You'd better show me this paper you've written as well.

89
00:03:50,167 --> 00:03:56,051
For a global competition such as this you need to make sure the technical details you've given are accurate

90
00:03:56,051 --> 00:03:56,670
and thorough.

91
00:03:56,670 --> 00:03:58,150
That would be a great help.

92
00:03:58,150 --> 00:04:00,040
Is there anything else I can do?

93
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:01,510
Well, I'm really....
